<<<
The reason why which has been called * impossible ’ is that what & which are of different grammatical values, which being a simple relative pronoun, while what (=that which, or a thing that) is a combination of antecedent & relative;
===
The reason why *which* has been called 'impossible' is that *what & which* are of different grammatical values, *which* being a simple relative pronoun, while *what* (=that which, or a thing that) is a combination of antecedent & relative;
>>>

<<<
but the second verb needs the antecedent-relative just as much as the first, if but or and is inserted; if neither but nor and is present, which will sometimes be possible, & so omission of but & and would be another cure for the last two examples.
===
but the second verb needs the antecedent-relative just as much as the first, if *but* or *and* is inserted; if neither *but* nor *and* is present, *which* will sometimes be possible, & so omission of *but* & *and* would be another cure for the last two examples.
>>>

